{
  "summary": "Katrafay (Cedrelopsis grevei) is a medicinal tree endemic to Madagascar, traditionally used for fatigue, muscle pain, and postpartum recovery. Its bark contains sesquiterpenes such as ishwarone, copaborneol, and cryptomerione, which contribute to its anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and tonic properties. Modern evidence supports its use as an adaptogenic and anti-fatigue agent, though clinical data remain limited.",
  "mechanism": "The sesquiterpenes in Katrafay, particularly ishwarone and copaborneol, are thought to inhibit pro-inflammatory cytokines (e.g., TNF-α, IL-6) and modulate the COX-2 pathway, providing analgesic and anti-inflammatory effects. Additionally, the bark's adaptogenic properties may involve modulation of the HPA axis and reduction of oxidative stress, contributing to its anti-fatigue action. Cryptomerione has been shown to activate TRPV1 receptors, which may explain its traditional use in pain relief.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Anti-fatigue",
      "evidence": "C",
      "note": "Limited pilot studies suggest improvement in physical endurance and reduced perceived exertion."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Anti-inflammatory",
      "evidence": "C",
      "note": "In vitro studies demonstrate inhibition of COX-2 and reduction of pro-inflammatory cytokines."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Analgesic",
      "evidence": "C",
      "note": "Animal models show dose-dependent pain relief in inflammatory pain assays."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Traditional use for postpartum recovery",
      "evidence": "trad",
      "note": "Widely used in Malagasy traditional medicine to support recovery after childbirth."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Tonic/adaptogen",
      "evidence": "C",
      "note": "Preliminary research indicates potential adaptogenic effects, possibly via HPA axis modulation."
    }
  ],
  "safetyNotes": [
    "Do not use during pregnancy or breastfeeding due to lack of safety data and potential uterine stimulant effects."

