{
  "summary": "Farfaraea officinalis, an alpine variant of coltsfoot, is traditionally used in European alpine medicine as an expectorant and demulcent for respiratory conditions. Its primary active compounds include mucilage, flavonoids, essential oils, and saponins, which contribute to its anti-inflammatory and soothing properties. However, due to potential pyrrolizidine alkaloid content, its use is contraindicated during pregnancy and prolonged administration.",
  "mechanism": "The mucilage content forms a protective film over the pharyngeal mucosa, providing demulcent relief from irritation and dry cough. Flavonoids such as quercetin and kaempferol inhibit cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase pathways, reducing inflammation in the respiratory tract. Essential oils and saponins may stimulate bronchial secretion and ciliary activity, enhancing expectoration. However, the presence of pyrrolizidine alkaloids poses a risk of hepatotoxicity with chronic use due to their metabolism to toxic pyrrole derivatives.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Expectorant for productive coughs",
      "evidence": "C",
      "note": "Limited clinical studies support traditional use; mucilage and saponins may enhance bronchial secretion."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Demulcent for throat irritation",
      "evidence": "trad",
      "note": "Based on traditional use in alpine medicine; mucilage content provides soothing effect."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Anti-inflammatory in respiratory mucosa",
      "evidence": "C",
      "note": "In vitro studies show flavonoid-mediated inhibition of pro-inflammatory cytokines and COX/LOX pathways."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Soothing for dry cough",
      "evidence": "trad",
      "note": "Traditional use as a demulcent; anecdotal reports support efficacy."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Potential antitussive",
      "evidence": "D",
      "note": "Anecdotal reports suggest cough-suppressant effects, but no clinical trials confirm this."
    }
  ]

